{
  "summary": "Competitor landscape (Indify, Apption, WidgetBox, Widgetly, Blocs, Widgetsmith, Notion Marketplace): nearly everyone clusters around the same 8-10 staples \u2014 clock, countdown, weather, quote, counter, life/year progress, habit, calendar, plus a Spotify/Google-Calendar embed. Indify covers ~10 guided widgets, Apption leans on third-party iframes (TradingView, CoinGecko, Albumizr), Blocs is the strongest on pure client-side productivity (pomodoro, water, habit), and Widgetsmith/iOS galleries push aesthetics. The big weaknesses: (1) shallow customization and dated visuals \u2014 most are utilitarian, not design-forward; (2) almost no Finance widgets beyond a crypto/stock iframe (no savings ring, budget, debt, subscriptions, net worth, expense splitter); (3) Health is fragmented across paid templates rather than embeddable widgets (water, sleep, calorie, BMI, fasting, breathing); (4) Dev/Creator is barely served beyond a GitHub heatmap and a subscriber counter; (5) Fun/Aesthetic ambient widgets (lava lamp, gradient mesh, starfield, dice, 8-ball) are essentially absent from the Notion-specific market. Where we win: a single cohesive design system with one shared {config, accent, s, theme} renderer means we can ship 50+ visually consistent, themeable widgets \u2014 and crucially we can own the pure-client-side long tail (math/Date/localStorage/SVG) that needs no backend, gating only the genuinely live-data ones (weather, tickers, GitHub, subscriber counts, calendar feeds) behind Pro. Priority 1 = high-value, pure-client, ship-first (progress bars, day counters, savings/budget rings, water, mood, breathing, dice, greeting). Priority 3 = anything needing an external API or heavy canvas work.",
